#
# prima-materia — Waidan, the external alchemy
#
# Opened on 2026-09-10 from the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi neipian, the
# chapter on the golden elixir. The transmission was registered and empty
# until then. Order of the file: the practice, the catalogue of capacities
# and the yielding claim, then the four places where the chapter commits
# itself to a number, then the two arguments it offers for why a mineral
# should work at all, then the conditions of failure, the cost, the
# dispute with the breathing chapter of the same book, and the one
# ascription of success it names.
#
# Held apart from traditions/daoist.ttl although both draw on the same work.
# That file holds the chapters on the circulation of the qi, on the mountain
# and on the guarding of the One; this one holds what the fourth chapter says
# against them.
#

pmt:Waidan
    skos:note "Read at the wording on 2026-09-10, in the Chinese: Baopuzi neipian 4, on the golden elixir, whole. The remaining chapters bearing on this transmission, above all 16 on gold and silver and 11 on the drugs of immortality, are searched by term and not read; the Zhouyi cantong qi is not opened. The harvest still owed at the opened work is recorded at prima-materia#633."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition note. Read in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, in the same digitisation as the chapters held in traditions/daoist.ttl, so that the two files cite one text and any divergence between them is a divergence in the work and not in the copy."@en ;
    skos:note "The finding of the opening run, and it is one sentence set against another in the same chapter. The author states that he received the elixir scriptures more than twenty years ago and has never compounded them, having not so much as a picul of means, and that all he has is a long sigh. Thirteen leaves later he prices the operation: about four hundred thousand cash for one preparation, enough for eight men. The chapter that calls the golden elixir the utmost of the way therefore rests, in its own author's hands, on an operation he could not afford to perform. Nothing in the file below is reported by a man who carried it out."@en .

#
# The practice and what it is said to confer
#

pmp:Jindan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Compounding and taking the golden elixir"@en, "Das Bereiten und Einnehmen des Goldelixiers"@de ;
    skos:definition "The compounding of mineral preparations and their ingestion, in the two forms the chapter calls the great essentials: the returned elixir, cycled in a sealed vessel over a graded fire for a counted number of days, and the golden liquor, in which a catty of gold and named accessory substances are laid in the flowery pool for a hundred days without fire until they turn to liquid. The chapter says it has surveyed more than a thousand juan of books on nourishing the nature and on long vision, and that none of them fails to take these two as the great essential."@en ;
    skos:note "The author's own commitment is unusually exposed and is quoted here because the whole file hangs on it: take these and fail to become an immortal, he says, and there have never been immortals since antiquity. A transmission that stakes its existence on one operation is easier to hold to account than one that does not, and this is the strongest such sentence located in the collection so far."@en ;
    skos:note "The two forms are not equally hard, and the chapter says which is which. The nine elixirs need many accessory drugs, to be had only while the four quarters are open and not when the realm is divided, and a fire kept up day and night for tens of days whose strength must never depart from the fit measure. For the golden liquor only the gold is hard to come by; no fire is raised at all, and the counted days in the pool are enough. The chapter recommends the second on grounds of feasibility and not of power."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

pmc:JindanCapacities a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "What the elixirs are said to confer"@en, "Was die Elixiere verleihen sollen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A catalogue in the enumerated form, and the items are of three kinds. Deathlessness: the taker does not age and does not die, his span becomes endless and ends with heaven and earth, and he rides the clouds and drives dragons up and down the Great Clarity. Powers over the world: walking on water with the elixir smeared under the feet; the destruction of the three corpses and the nine worms; the healing of the hundred illnesses; warding off the five weapons, and off thieves, tigers and wolves; the attendance of jade women and of the spirits of mountains and rivers, seen in human shape; the raising of a man dead not more than three days, by washing him with the blue elixir in water and putting a knife-point of it in his mouth; and a man of ninety fathering children. Transmutation: a knife-point of the elixir with a catty of mercury makes silver, and with the fire it makes gold, and vessels made of that gold cause whoever eats from them to live long."@en ;
    skos:note "The catalogue is what the registration of this transmission was made for: it is an explicit list of attainments in the same enumerated form as the siddhi lists of the Indic material this collection holds, compiled without access to them. Whether the resemblance is a route or a form is not decided here and is not to be decided by resemblance."@en ;
    skos:note "The three kinds are not equally reachable by an examination, and the difference is in the record rather than smoothed away. The raising of the dead and the fathering of children at ninety are outcomes a witness could report; the walking on water is a public event; the endless span and the attendance of jade women are not, in this form. The transmutation of mercury into silver is the only item on the list that names a material operation with a visible product, and the chapter uses exactly that item as its assay."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

pmc:JindanYieldsCapacities a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The golden elixir yields those capacities"@en, "Das Goldelixier bringt jene Vermögen hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Jindan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JindanCapacities ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

#
# The four places where the chapter commits itself to a number
#

pmc:JiudanMingshu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The nine elixirs, each with its name, its dose and its term"@en, "Die neun Elixiere, jedes mit Namen, Gabe und Frist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Nine preparations are named in order, and each carries a term. The first, Cinnabar Flower, is made in thirty-six days and taken; in seven days one is an immortal. The second, Divine Elixir, also called Divine Tally: in a hundred days; three knife-points destroy the three corpses and the nine worms and heal the hundred illnesses, and smeared under the feet it carries one over water. The third, also Divine Elixir: one knife-point, a hundred days, and jade women and the spirits of mountains and rivers attend in human shape; given to the six domestic animals they too do not die. The fourth, Returned Elixir: one knife-point, a hundred days; written with it above a man's eyes, the hundred demons flee. The fifth, Bait Elixir: thirty days. The sixth, Smelted Elixir: ten days. The seventh, Pliant Elixir: one knife-point, a hundred days, and taken with the juice of quepen it lets a man of ninety father children. The eighth, Prostrate Elixir: the same day. The ninth, Cold Elixir: one knife-point, a hundred days, and one flies without wings. Of these nine, the chapter says, one need only obtain a single one to become an immortal; making all of them is a matter of preference and not of necessity."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause that any one of the nine suffices is what makes the list examinable rather than a counsel of perfection. A transmission that required the whole set could meet every failure by naming the elixir that was not made; this one has closed that exit in advance, in its own words."@en ;
    skos:note "The terms run from the same day to a hundred days, and the ordering of the list does not follow them. The eighth acts on the day it is taken and the ninth takes a hundred, so the sequence is not a scale of potency, and whatever orders the nine is not stated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

pmc:JiuzhuanRishu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The nine cyclings and the days they save"@en, "Die neun Umwandlungen und die Tage, die sie sparen"@de ;
    skos:definition "For the Great Clarity elixir the chapter gives a table in which the labour of preparation and the wait after taking stand in inverse proportion. The elixir of one cycling, taken, gives immortality in three years; of two cyclings, in two years; of three, in one year; of four, in half a year; of five, in a hundred days; of six, in forty days; of seven, in thirty days; of eight, in ten days; of nine, in three days. The chapter then states the reason: where the cyclings are few the days of firing are many and the drug's force is not sufficient, so that taking it uses many days and immortality comes slowly; where the cyclings are many the drug's force is complete, so that taking it uses few days and immortality comes fast."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most quantitatively committed claim located in this collection. It is not a list of outcomes but a stated law relating a preparation variable to an interval, with a mechanism given for the relation, and both ends of the scale named. Anything of this shape can be got wrong in a way that a catalogue of capacities cannot."@en ;
    skos:note "The relation is stated as inverse but not as proportional, and the numbers are not. Three years, two years, one year, half a year, a hundred days, forty, thirty, ten, three: the fall is steep at the top and shallow in the middle, and no rule generates it. What the chapter offers as a reason accounts for the direction of the table and not for its values."@en ;
    skos:note "The same passage carries the chapter's own answer to the charge of empty writing: were these empty writings, it asks, how could the nine cyclings and nine transformations come out by the counted days exactly as the recipe says. The check offered is therefore the agreement of the process with its own timetable and not the arrival of the outcome, which is a lower bar than the one the table itself sets."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

pmc:JinyeFenliang a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The golden liquor, by the ounce"@en, "Der Goldsaft, nach Unzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "For the golden liquor the dose is graded and each grade has its own outcome. A hundred days of compounding make it. One ounce taken, and one is an immortal. Half an ounce, and one has long life without death, and the ten thousand harms and the hundred poisons cannot injure; and at that grade one may keep a wife and children, hold office and rank, and do as one wishes, with nothing forbidden. Whoever then wishes to ascend fasts again and takes the further ounce, and flies off. Whoever wishes to ascend to heaven at once must first cut off grain for a year before taking it. The scripture adds a sign: when the golden liquor enters the mouth, the body becomes gold in colour."@en ;
    skos:note "The half-ounce grade is the part of this file that a modern reader will most easily misplace. It does not describe a lesser immortal but a stated condition in which the ordinary life goes on unchanged and no prohibition applies. A transmission that names a dose at which its own discipline lapses is saying something about what the discipline was for, and it is the opposite of what the rest of the chapter demands."@en ;
    skos:note "The colour of the body is the only immediately observable sign the chapter names for any of its preparations. Everything else is a term to be waited out; this one is a report a bystander could make on the day. It is also the item most easily produced by the preparation itself rather than by any effect of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

pmc:JinchengYaocheng a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "If the gold forms, the drug is finished"@en, "Ist das Gold entstanden, ist das Mittel fertig"@de ;
    skos:definition "The assay the chapter gives for the first elixir, and with it for the operation as a whole. Two hundred and forty zhu of the elixir joined with a hundred catties of mercury and put to the fire make gold. Where the gold forms, the drug is complete. Where the gold does not form, the drug is to be sealed again and fired again for the same count of days, and then, the chapter says, there is no case in which it does not form."@en ;
    skos:note "The two halves of this sentence do different work and the difference is the reason for the node. The first half is a criterion: a visible product decides whether an invisible one is finished, which is more than most of the material this collection holds ever offers. The second half withdraws it, because a failure that requires only repetition is not an outcome the operation can have. What remains after both halves is a rule under which no run ever counts against the recipe."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the same structure the collection holds from the burial book and from the treatise against the year and month taboos, and it arrives here from the inside rather than from a critic. Nobody is being answered; the immunising clause is printed as part of the instructions."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

#
# The two arguments for why a mineral should work at all
#

pmc:DanshaHuanfan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Cinnabar goes to mercury and comes back"@en, "Zinnober wird zu Quecksilber und kehrt zurück"@de ;
    skos:definition "The observation on which the chapter rests its case for minerals over plants. Grasses and trees, burnt, are ash at once; cinnabar, burnt, becomes mercury, and by accumulated transformation it becomes cinnabar again. Therefore, the chapter argues, it is far removed from common plants and can make a man long-lived. It adds that the common people do not know that mercury comes out of cinnabar, and that told so they will not believe it, objecting that cinnabar is a red thing and asking whence it should become this white thing, and that cinnabar is a stone, and that burnt stones all become ash."@en ;
    skos:note "The observation is correct and repeatable, which is rare in this collection and is what makes the passage worth a node of its own. What it does not carry is the step: a substance that returns to its own form after transformation is not thereby a substance that keeps a body from returning to earth. The chapter states the observation and the conclusion and leaves the step between them unstated."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter also reports the objection it met, and the objection is the same kind of argument as its own: cinnabar is a stone, and stones burn to ash. Both sides reason from what a substance does in the fire to what it will do in a man. The one that happened to have looked was right about the fire."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

pmc:JiaqiuWaiwu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Firmness borrowed from an outer thing"@en, "Festigkeit, von einem äußeren Ding geborgt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The mechanism the chapter states. Gold entering the fire is not consumed by a hundred smeltings, and buried to the end of heaven it does not decay; the golden elixir, the longer it is burnt the finer its transformations. Taking these two drugs refines the body, and so makes a man neither age nor die. This, the chapter says expressly, is to seek from an outer thing in order to make oneself firm: as fat feeds a fire so that it does not go out, and as copper-green smeared on the feet keeps them from rotting in water, borrowing the strength of the copper to guard the flesh. But the elixir, entering the body, soaks the constructive and the defensive qi, and is not merely an outward application like the copper-green."@en ;
    skos:note "The argument is a transfer of a property, and it names its own weakest point in the act of making it. Both illustrations are of surfaces: the fat is outside the flame it feeds, the copper-green outside the foot it guards. The chapter sees this and answers it by saying that the elixir goes inside, which asserts the disanalogy it was asked about rather than removing it."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the claim that the whole file rests on, and it is the one place where an examination could take hold without waiting out a term: it says that an ingested mineral confers on tissue the incorruptibility the mineral has in fire and in earth. It is stated as a mechanism and not as a report, and nothing in the chapter offers a case of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

#
# What is said to prevent it, and what it costs
#

pmc:HedanTiaojian a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The conditions under which the elixir will not form"@en, "Die Bedingungen, unter denen das Elixier nicht entsteht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The compounding is to be done in a famous mountain, in a place without people, with companions not exceeding three; with a fast of a hundred days beforehand and washing in the five fragrances; with no approach to pollution and no coming and going with common people. Beyond that the chapter names two conditions that lie outside the operator's hands. Unbelievers must not be let to know of it, for if they slander the divine drug the drug will not form. And whoever has not the bone of an immortal cannot so much as get sight of this way. The transmission itself is bound by an oath: a gold man and a gold fish, or a gold man of eight ounces, are thrown into an eastward-flowing stream and blood is smeared for the covenant, and whoever steals the recipe and works it without the proper method will never succeed. Before the gold that the elixir makes may be used, eighty-eight catties of it are to be sacrificed to named powers, and twelve more put in a good leather bag and abandoned in silence in a crowded market on a good day, walking away without looking back; whoever uses the gold before sacrificing it will certainly meet with calamity."@en ;
    skos:note "Three of these conditions cannot be checked before the attempt and cannot be cleared after it. Whether an unbeliever heard of the work, whether anyone slandered it, and whether the operator has the bone of an immortal are all settled by whether the elixir formed. Together with the reseal-and-refire rule at the assay, they leave the recipe with no way of being wrong."@en ;
    skos:note "The prescribed sacrifice of gold stands in the same chapter as the sentence that the way of long life is not in sacrifice and in serving ghosts and spirits. Both are printed as instructions and neither is qualified. The file records the contradiction rather than choosing a side, because a transmission that carries both is telling something about how it was assembled."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Jindan ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

pmc:JindanFeiyong a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "What immortality costs, and the smallest batch that works"@en, "Was die Unsterblichkeit kostet, und die kleinste Menge, die gelingt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prices the operation. For the golden liquor only the gold is hard to get: a catty by the old weight is two catties by the present one and costs not more than some three hundred thousand; the accessory drugs are easier to assemble, and no fire is raised, so that the days in the flowery pool suffice. Altogether about four hundred thousand yields one preparation, and one preparation is enough to make eight men immortal. Against that stands a rule of scale: those who compound somewhat less than the stated amount find that its force is not sufficient to effect the transformation, as brewing a few sheng of rice will certainly yield no wine."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated price is a rare thing to find in this collection and it does two things at once. It puts the operation inside the economy, where it can be reached by anyone with the sum, and it explains why the author of the chapter never reached it. He says elsewhere in the same chapter that he received the scriptures more than twenty years ago and had not so much as a picul of means to do it with."@en ;
    skos:note "The minimum-batch rule closes the cheapest way to attempt the operation and does so with an analogy that is true of brewing. It is also a second condition under which a failed run says nothing about the recipe, since a run that failed can be assigned to a batch that was too small."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .

#
# The chapter against the breathing chapter of the same book
#

pmc:ShengxianZaiShendan a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the essential lies in the breath or in the elixir"@en, "Ob das Entscheidende im Atem liegt oder im Elixier"@de ;
    skos:definition "One book holds both sides and the fourth chapter states its own plainly: the way of long life is not in sacrifices and the serving of ghosts and spirits, and not in guiding and pulling and in bending and stretching; the essential of ascending as an immortal is in the divine elixir. It says the same in another form at the outset, that though breathing and guiding-and-pulling and the taking of drugs from grasses and trees may lengthen the years, they do not exempt from death, whereas taking the divine elixir makes a man's span endless and ending only with heaven and earth. And it names a man who went the other way: Wang Tu, a military supervisor of the Wei, who knew nothing of the great drug and wanted to seek immortality by circulating the qi and entering the chamber, and made a book saying the way was complete in that, which the chapter calls a grave misleading of men. The eighth chapter of the same book, held in traditions/daoist.ttl, gives the circulation of the qi its own catalogue of capacities and names it among the essentials."@en ;
    skos:note "It is not a disagreement between schools but between two chapters written by one man, which makes it worth a node rather than a remark. Whoever cites the Baopuzi for the circulation of the qi is citing a book that says elsewhere the circulation does not exempt from death, and whoever cites it for the elixir is citing a book that also teaches the breathing at length."@en ;
    skos:note "The two chapters can be reconciled, and the fourth does the reconciling itself: the lesser methods are to be used to keep oneself going while the great drug is out of reach, since it is hard to bring off at short notice. That leaves the circulation of the qi as a holding measure and not as a way, which is a demotion the eighth chapter nowhere states."@en ;
    skos:note "The demotion has a plain material cause and the file records it beside the doctrine. The great drug costs four hundred thousand and the breathing costs nothing. A ranking that puts the expensive operation at the top and the free one in the waiting room is not thereby wrong, but it is the ranking the author himself could not act on."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:XingqiYieldsCapacities ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4", "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8" .

#
# The one success the chapter names
#

pmc:YinjunDexian a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "The scholar of Xinye who is said to have got it by this elixir"@en, "Der Gelehrte aus Xinye, der es durch dieses Elixier erlangt haben soll"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names one man. Lord Yin of Xinye, in recent times at the end of the Han, obtained immortality by this Great Clarity elixir. He was by origin a Confucian scholar, of talent and reflection, and wrote poems and a eulogy on the elixir scriptures with a preface, setting out the beginning and end of his own study under his teacher and listing the men he knew of who had obtained immortality, more than forty of them, very clearly."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the whole of the evidence the chapter offers that anybody ever succeeded, and its shape is worth keeping. The immortality of the one man is asserted; the forty are a list he is said to have made, reported at second hand by an author who did not perform the operation himself. What is offered as confirmation is therefore a document about a document, and the chapter presents it as very clear."@en ;
    skos:note "The one detail that would count differently is that the man is said to have written down the beginning and end of his own study under a named teacher. An account of an operation by the person who ran it is the thing this collection everywhere lacks, and this node records that such a document is named as having existed."@en ;
    pm:ascribedTo "Lord Yin of Xinye, a Confucian scholar of the end of the Han" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:JindanCapacities ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" .
